This will be based on your personas desire, partially informed by your CVPs, and will typically dictate the direction for narrative, perspective, and tone. So figuring these out is core to building your 'body' blocks.

Start generic, and work your way into niche iterations that are truly built for your brand. 

Let's start from the top:
* **Curiosity** You're focusing on a topic or item that the viewer is either unfamiliar with or wants to learn more about
* **Discovery** You're helping the viewer find or understand why they should try something new
* **Value** You're communicating price, savings, or holistic worth of your product/service. This doesn't always have to mean best value, it can mean better value. It also doesn't have to mean heavily discounted or cheap. Time, energy, emotional capacity are all valuable ways to save.
* **Range** You're elaborating on the full offerings of your brand or service to shape or expand awareness. I think this could also be applied to niches within a brand/product/service, for instance "we have something for for every budget" or "reviewing each of our blush lines" 
* **Convenience** You're showing and telling how your brand/product/service makes something easier or more accessible for the viewer
* **Perception Shift** You're aiming to change the current beliefs of a viewer by educating them on the reality, as it pertains to your product/service/brand
* **Comparison** One of those malleable framings that could be a hook or an angle or the format of the body. So, what does it mean here? The angle you're coming at, and this will largely be determined by the persona, will show them how your product/brand/service operates as opposed to one they already use/might be considering/tried in the past. This could be a method or assumption, rather than a literal competitor.
* **Aspiration** You're trying to speak to an audience that is actively working on self-improvement, growth, or status.
* **Relatability** Some angles don't have to be based on tension. Some people are moved by connection, and if you can help them see the way the brand fits their personalities or lifestyles, then that can function as an effective pitch. This can suit vlog-style videos (i.e., What I eat in a day, Come with me) that center your brand/service. 
* **Location** Depending on the industry, service, or business, a local angle could make a huge impact. This is relevant in the food and grocery industry, but could apply across the board if you have a relevant spin for it.
